{"name":"Cork Wedge","hex":"#c1a98a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cork","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The dead protective tissue between the bark and cambium in woody plants, with suberin deposits making it impervious to gasses and water.","The phellem of the cork oak, used for making bottle stoppers, flotation devices, and insulation material."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To seal or stop up, especially with a cork stopper.","To blacken (as) with a burnt cork."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cork"},{"term":"wedge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the simple machines; a piece of material, such as metal or wood, thick at one edge and tapered to a thin edge at the other for insertion in a narrow crevice, used for splitting, tightening, securing, or levering.","A piece (of food, metal, wood etc.) having this shape."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To support or secure using a wedge.","To force into a narrow gap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wedge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wedge","blocks":[{"text":"A wedge is a triangular shaped tool, a portable inclined plane, and one of the six simple machines. It can be used to separate two objects or portions of an object, lift up an object, or hold an object in place. It functions by converting a force applied to its blunt end into forces perpendicular (normal) to its inclined surfaces. The mechanical advantage of a wedge is given by the ratio of the length of its slope to its width."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wedge","wikidata":"Q131408"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Wedge-1.jpg/500px-Wedge-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Wedge-1.jpg/1280px-Wedge-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/